    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Truganini, a member of the Nuenonne people, saw her mother murdered by sailors and two of her sisters kidnapped by a sealer, and in her teens and twenties lived through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a> between colonists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a>.</li><li>From 1830 she guided George Augustus Robinson on expeditions that ended in the exile of the Aboriginal people of the Tasmanian mainland, and she was herself exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a> in 1835.</li><li>After her death the colonial government declared the island's Aboriginal population extinct, a claim scholars and the Aboriginal Tasmanian community reject, and her remains were displayed for decades against her wishes before being cremated and scattered in 1976.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Truganini twice saved Robinson's life by swimming across the Arthur River with him or towing his raft, in 1832 and 1834, yet she begged her doctor that her body not be mutilated by scientists after her death.</p></aside>
    </section>

