    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1812</span><span class="rf-text">Truganini is born at Recherche Bay, the youngest daughter of Manganerer of the Nuenonne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1829</span><span class="rf-text">Robinson brings her to his mission at Missionary Bay on Bruny Island, and in October she marries Woureddy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text">She sets out as a guide on Robinson's &quot;friendly mission&quot; to the Aboriginal peoples of western and north-western Tasmania</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1835</span><span class="rf-text">Robinson announces that the Aboriginal population of the Tasmanian mainland has been exiled, and Truganini is taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1841</span><span class="rf-text">She is tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> with four others and acquitted as an accessory; Maulboyheenner and Peevay are convicted and hanged in January 1842</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1847</span><span class="rf-text">The survivors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a>, Truganini among them, are moved to Oyster Cove</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1876</span><span class="rf-text">Truganini dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a>, and the Tasmanian government declares the island's Aboriginal population extinct</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">Her remains, held by the Tasmanian Museum, are cremated and scattered in the D'Entrecasteaux Channel</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="James Cook" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captainjamescookportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nathaniel Dance-Holland, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Truganini was born around 1812 at Recherche Bay in southern Tasmania, then known as Van Diemen's Land. She was the youngest daughter of Manganerer, the senior man of the Nuenonne clan, whose country included Bruny Island and the coastal area of the Tasmanian mainland between Recherche Bay and Oyster Cove. Her mother, with whom Manganerer had at least three daughters, was likely a member of the Ninene people. At the beginning of the 19th century the Aboriginal population of Tasmania numbered around 3000–8000, forming nine nations divided into around 50–100 clan groups.</p><p>British colonisation had reached Nuenonne country before her birth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> first landed on Bruny Island at Adventure Bay in 1777, and within a few decades former convicts were raiding Aboriginal communities to kidnap women. When French explorers and scientists visited in 1802, the Nuenonne they met were terrified of their guns. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> was founded in 1804, many ships passed Nuenonne country. The seal colonies the Nuenonne relied on were soon destroyed, and many Nuenonne women were forced to trade sex for food with settlers at the whaling stations. In 1816 sailors murdered Truganini's mother, and in 1826 a sealer kidnapped two of her sisters.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Truganini&#8221; cites where it covers Early life and people:</p><ul><li>Cassandra Pybus, Truganini: Journey Through the Apocalypse (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2020)</li><li>Lyndall Ryan, Tasmanian Aborigines: A History Since 1803 (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-arthur.jpg" alt="George Arthur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Major-General_Sir_George_Arthur_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the late 1820s Tasmania was in the midst of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a>, a conflict between colonists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> in which kidnapping of Aboriginal women was common and displaced Aboriginal people carried out retributive violence. In 1819 the Aboriginal and settler populations had each been around 5000, the settlers overwhelmingly male; by 1830 the settler population had grown to 23,500. According to an unverified account published shortly before Truganini's death by the surveyor James Erskine Calder, around 1828 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> cutters abducted and raped her and murdered two Nuenonne men, one of them her fiancé Paraweena.</p><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a> declared martial law in the settled districts in November 1828, but not on Bruny Island, which he thought a suitable site for conciliation. He appointed George Augustus Robinson, a builder from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and a devout Christian who expressed religious and humanitarian motivations, to run a ration station there. Robinson met Truganini in April 1829 among convict woodcutters on the mainland and brought her to a mission at Missionary Bay, where he used her presence to draw her father and others. Disease soon struck the mission. Truganini left for the whaling station at Adventure Bay, but Robinson retrieved her, and after initial firm resistance she reluctantly married the Nuenonne elder Woureddy in October 1829.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In January 1830 Robinson obtained the governor's approval for a "friendly mission" to the Aboriginal peoples of western and north-western Tasmania, and Truganini went with him as a guide. The party set out from Recherche Bay on 3 February 1830. Truganini, who was suffering from advanced syphilis, collected food by diving for shellfish and gathering plants. On the way they learned of massacres in the Black War, in which about 60 settlers and 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> had been killed over the preceding two years. Tom Lawson has described the expeditions, which continued until 1834, as a "roving embassy" that eventually negotiated an end to the violent conflict.</p><p>As the governor's "Black Line" militia took the field, Robinson set out with Truganini and others to persuade groups to surrender, and they convinced some, among them the warrior Mannalargenna's group, to go to Swan Island, an exposed island with little food or clean water. Robinson later settled Aboriginal people on Gun Carriage Island and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a>, over the guides' complaints. In 1832 and 1834 Truganini saved Robinson's life at the Arthur River. In 1833 she led parties that located Ninine and Tarkiner people; many of those brought to Sarah Island died, and only a handful were alive when they were sent to Flinders Island on 20 November 1833.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Truganini and the other guides were often at odds with Robinson. They complained about being resettled on Gun Carriage Island, and Truganini refused to enter her cottage there and begged to return to the mainland. Robinson was rewarded with land grants and hundreds of pounds, while the guides received clothing and a boat that they never saw or used; Robinson rented it out and kept the proceeds in an account he controlled. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> he changed residents' names and made them wear European clothes, renaming Truganini "Lalla Rookh". Of the 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> who had surrendered to Robinson since 1830, 112 were alive at Wybalenna in 1835, and about half of the residents died between 1835 and 1839, mostly of pneumonia.</p><p>In 1841, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-phillip-district">Port Phillip District</a>, Truganini ran away from the Aboriginal encampment with Maulboyheenner, Peevay and two women. They plundered a settler's hut, and Maulboyheenner and Peevay beat and shot two whalers to death, mistaking them for members of a search party. The five were tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>; as non-Christians they could not testify, and their counsel Redmond Barry argued that it was unfair to try an "alien people" in an unfamiliar courtroom. Truganini and the other women were acquitted; the two men were hanged on 20 January 1842 before a crowd of 4000–5000. In 1976 Vivienne Rae-Ellis published a biography presenting Truganini as a traitor who collaborated with settlers, a view other writers have contested.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Truganini&#8221; cites where it covers Port Phillip District and trial:</p><ul><li>Cassandra Pybus, Truganini: Journey Through the Apocalypse (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2020)</li><li>Murray Johnson, Van Diemen's Land: An Aboriginal History (University of New South Wales Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-victoria.jpg" alt="Queen Victoria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Victoria_by_Bassano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Bassano, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Truganini was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> in July 1842; Woureddy died on the journey, likely of syphilis. The superintendent forced her into a marriage with Mannapackername, and both rebelled. After the residents petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and the governor, the settlement was closed in May 1847, and the 47 survivors were moved to Oyster Cove on 18 October 1847. There Truganini hunted marsupials, dived for shellfish and often returned to Bruny Island. Mortality was high, in part because of alcohol, and the residents were given mercury chloride, which likely contributed to mental illness. By 1859 only 14 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> were left, and in 1861 her clanswoman Dray died.</p><p>Museums and collectors began gathering remains from the settlement, and the survivors were frequently photographed. William Lanne, her partner, who was understood to be the last surviving Aboriginal Tasmanian man, died on 3 March 1869, and members of the Royal Society mutilated his body to secure his skeleton. Truganini begged a minister to see that she was buried at sea. Moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> in 1872 and cared for by the Dandridge family, she asked her doctor to have her buried "behind the mountains". She fell into a coma on 4 May 1876 and died on 8 May. Her body was buried at the Female Factory to protect it from body snatchers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Truganini&#8221; cites where it covers Wybalenna and final expedition, Death, Remains and repatriation:</p><ul><li>Cassandra Pybus, Truganini: Journey Through the Apocalypse (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2020)</li><li>Lyndall Ryan, Tasmanian Aborigines: A History Since 1803 (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2012)</li><li>Murray Johnson, Van Diemen's Land: An Aboriginal History (University of New South Wales Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After her death the Royal Society's Secretary asked for her remains, and the government permitted only a cast of her face. In 1878, after a campaign by the society, her body was disinterred for use only in science, and about a decade later her skull was shown at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> Centennial Exhibition. In 1904 an articulated skeleton was made for display at the Tasmanian Museum. It was withdrawn from view in 1947 after campaigning, legislation in 1975 gave the government ownership, and her remains were cremated and scattered in the D'Entrecasteaux Channel in 1976. Skin and hair were returned in 2002 and 2005.</p><p>Truganini was widely, though incorrectly, described as the last surviving Aboriginal Tasmanian. Scholars and the present Aboriginal Tasmanian community reject that claim: a community descended from Aboriginal women and sealers continued on Cape Barren Island and elsewhere in the Furneaux Group, and Lyndall Ryan's 1981 book was among the first to challenge the extinction myth. Tom Lawson argues that the narrative served to reinforce British power. Clive Turnbull in 1948 portrayed her as resisting the extermination of her people; Rebe Taylor wrote that she became a symbol of white Australian guilt. Some Aboriginal people reclaim her as an anti-colonial figure. In 2009 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> stopped the sale at Sotheby's of copies of Benjamin Law's busts of her and Woureddy.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Truganini&#8221; cites where it covers Extinction myth, Cultural depictions:</p><ul><li>Rebe Taylor, Into the Heart of Tasmania: A Search for Human Antiquity (Melbourne University Publishing, 2017)</li><li>Cassandra Pybus, Truganini: Journey Through the Apocalypse (Allen &amp; Unwin, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Truganini (c. 1812–1876), a Nuenonne woman from Bruny Island, guided George Augustus Robinson's expeditions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a> and was exiled with her people. She was wrongly called the last Aboriginal Tasmanian, a claim scholars and the Aboriginal Tasmanian community reject.</em></p>

